# Line endings are a correctness concern in this repository, not a style preference.
#
# Every file here was verified on Windows in the byte shape listed below. A clone on a machine with
# a different `core.autocrlf` must produce the same bytes, or the installer stops being the thing
# that was tested — a batch file that gains LF endings is a batch file nobody ran.

# Default: LF in the repository and in the working tree. This matches the shell scripts, the
# JavaScript, the JSON and the Markdown as they were tested.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Batch files are the entry point a user double-clicks, so they stay CRLF on every platform.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# The plain-text quick start is meant to be opened by Notepad from the extracted folder.
*.txt text eol=crlf

# PowerShell scripts keep whatever they are committed with; the UTF-8 BOM that Windows PowerShell
# 5.1 needs to read them is part of the committed blob and is never stripped by these rules.
*.ps1 text

# Already-compressed or generated payloads: never touched.
*.zip binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
